Q: How can I render an ASP.NET MVC view as a string? A: <p>The easiest way for me was:</p>
<pre><code>public string GetFileAsString(string path)
{
    var html = &quot;&quot;;

    FileStream fileStream = new FileStream(path, FileMode.Open);

    using (StreamReader reader = new StreamReader(fileStream))
    {
        html += reader.ReadLine();
    }

    return html;
}
</code></pre>
<p>I use this for emails and make sure that the file only contains CSS and HTML.</p>

Q: Swift 6 strict concurrency: How to achieve Sendable conformance for a class bridging C callbacks? A: <p>For macOS 15+, you can use Swift's <code>Mutex</code> to protect mutable properties like <code>_currentFingerCount</code>. Swift knows that <code>Mutex</code> is <code>Sendable</code>, so <code>MultitouchManager</code> can conform to <code>Sendable</code> without <code>@unchecked</code>.</p>
<pre><code>import Synchronization

final class MultitouchManager: Sendable {
    private let _currentFingerCount = Mutex(0)

    var currentFingerCount: Int {
        get {
            _currentFingerCount.withLock { $0 }
        }
        set {
            _currentFingerCount.withLock { $0 = newValue }
        }
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>If you have multiple properties that you want to protect with the same mutex, group them into a <code>struct</code>/<code>class</code> first,</p>
<pre><code>final class MultipleProperties {
    var x = 0
    var y = 0
    var z = 0
}

// ...

private let mutex = Mutex(MultipleProperties())
</code></pre>
